1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,383
JAMIE: Meta just trained an AI mode that mines your Facebook history, and A M D jumped nearly seven percent on the news.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/950264/meta-ai-mode-search-facebook

2
00:00:07,383 --> 00:00:15,729
ALEX: SpaceX's IPO raised ten billion more than the initial ask, which means Elon's now sitting on significant cash to pursue acquisitions without needing board permission.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgrk21wnvy9o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

3
00:00:15,729 --> 00:00:37,557
ALEX: Markets rallied with Nasdaq up three point zero-seven percent, S&P up one point six-five, and the V I X collapsed eight point three-seven percent. A M D posted a six point nine-eight percent jump. Meanwhile, energy got demolished. Exxon down four point one-four percent, Chevron down three point six-four percent. It's the kind of lopsided day where the market's basically saying: AI stocks forever, fossil fuels never.

4
00:00:37,557 --> 00:00:43,335
JAMIE: So the V I X dropped eight percent and nobody's even worried about what happens next week?

5
00:00:43,335 --> 00:01:00,027
MAYA: No macro catalyst, no Fed announcement, no earnings surprise—just pure algorithmic muscle memory pulling capital into the places it was already going. It's like watching someone eat cereal out of the box while driving because they're too committed to the habit to stop and think about whether they're actually hungry anymore.

6
00:01:00,027 --> 00:01:12,867
MAYA: Meta's letting you search across your own history through this AI Mode interface, and the AI is trained on public information to surface answers. It's like having a search engine that knows your embarrassing era better than you do.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/950264/meta-ai-mode-search-facebook

7
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:19,287
JAMIE: But Meta already has that data. Why does it matter that they're now wrapping it in an AI layer?

8
00:01:19,287 --> 00:01:32,449
ALEX: Because opt-in becomes a fairy tale once the AI's already trained. They're not asking permission to ingest your posts—they're announcing the product after the model's already learned from them. The permission window closed before anyone knew there was a window.

9
00:01:32,449 --> 00:01:49,462
MAYA: And here's what kills me: Meta's doing this while Europe's still writing rules about AI training data, and the U.S. regulators are basically speed-dating each other trying to figure out who gets to write the memo. By the time anyone drafts a policy, Meta's already three products deep into the same playbook.

10
00:01:49,462 --> 00:02:02,944
MAYA: SpaceX just closed its IPO, which means Elon's now a public company CEO for the first time, and that comes with quarterly earnings calls, investor letters, actual SEC filings where you can't just post a meme instead of answering the question.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/15/spacex-is-public-everything-you-need-to-know-post-ipo/

11
00:02:02,944 --> 00:02:18,673
ALEX: The greenshoe exercise worked exactly as designed. Underwriters took the extra demand and printed more shares at the offering price, so Elon walked away with that buffer. He's now got enough dry powder to move on every acquisition he's been eyeing without asking a board for permission again.

12
00:02:18,673 --> 00:02:27,019
JAMIE: So what happens in three months when SpaceX has to report earnings and the question is 'how many Starship launches actually made money this quarter?'

13
00:02:27,019 --> 00:02:38,575
ALEX: That's when the public markets discover that launching rockets on spec for the government doesn't have the same margin profile as selling cloud services. The 10-Q is going to be a real moment of truth.

14
00:02:38,575 --> 00:02:49,489
JAMIE: Trump's already floating Kevin Warsh as the next Fed Chair—and the story is less about monetary policy expertise and more about pre-positioning someone who trusts Trump before the rate-cut conversation gets politically urgent.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/15/trump-warsh-fed-reshape.html

15
00:02:49,489 --> 00:03:07,787
ALEX: Warsh is a serious operator, but the timing matters. Trump's signaling this now so that when the economy softens and rate cuts become inevitable, there's already a chair in place who won't treat the Fed's independence as a reason to say no. It's not about finding the best mind—it's about finding a mind that won't argue.

16
00:03:07,787 --> 00:03:17,417
MAYA: Trump says the Iran deal is already signed and details come pretty soon—which is geopolitical speak for 'I have a piece of paper but I'm still workshopping the ending.'
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/06/15/trump-iran-deal-questions-strait-funds

17
00:03:17,417 --> 00:03:33,467
JAMIE: Fox just paid twenty-two billion dollars for Roku—which is like buying a house because you fell in love with the smart thermostat. The bet is that linear T V's future is on streaming hardware, but you're really just buying a platform to run ads on someone else's living room.

18
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:49,838
MAYA: A V A R official made a hand gesture during a match and then claimed it was an involuntary, subconscious twitch—completely unaware it happened, had no intention to communicate anything. The investigation is ongoing, but the official is basically asking us to believe their hand has its own union rep.

19
00:03:49,838 --> 00:04:02,999
ALEX: So the defense is that their body committed a gesture without consulting their brain first. That's the line. And somewhere in a disciplinary hearing, a lawyer is actually arguing that muscle memory can't be held accountable for its own expressions.

20
00:04:02,999 --> 00:04:15,518
JAMIE: Every ref who's ever made a terrible call just found their legal precedent. 'Your Honor, I threw the flag involuntarily. My arm and I have a complicated relationship.' This person's handed every whistleblower in sports a permission structure.

21
00:04:15,518 --> 00:04:22,580
MAYA: Meta's betting that mining your past self for content recommendations feels less creepy once you're the one searching instead of them.

22
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:27,074
ALEX: Rebranding surveillance as self-service doesn't change what's being watched—just who holds the remote.

23
00:04:27,074 --> 00:04:31,568
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
